by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-17T09:33:52Z

Moved `createBoundaries()` from `UlidFactory` to `Ulid`, so the three types are consistent. The split shape was mine, and nothing in the method needs a configured factory.

The interface is back, on the ids instead of the factories: `TimeOrderedUidInterface extends TimeBasedUidInterface`, implemented by `Ulid`, `UuidV6` and `UuidV7`. Sorting in timestamp order is a property of the id type, not of what a factory is configured to create, so the conditional semantics that made me drop the idea do not arise. `UuidV1` keeps `TimeBasedUidInterface` alone.

The bounds are now computed from the timestamp instead of from a created id. Going through `generate()` had a side effect: it keeps a monotonic clock in a static, so bounds for a future date moved that clock and the ids created next carried the future date.

```php
UuidV7::createBoundaries(new \DateTimeImmutable('@4102444800'));
new UuidV7()->getDateTime(); // 2100-01-01 00:00:00.000806
```

A test pins that now. The bounds themselves are unchanged, byte for byte, from the epoch to year 9999.

